    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Selective visual attention has previously been studied only in humans and non-human primates. I have measured its effects in chickens. The effect of attention on perception can be measured by shape discrimination of various stimuli. When multiple stimuli are present, animals differentially process selected stimuli known as targets and suppress stimuli known as distracters. In my thesis research, I studied how the ability of chickens to attend to and identify a specific target was affected by the presence of a distracter at various locations. Behavioral tests, measured by gaze direction and reaction times, quantified shape discrimination. Three birds were trained to discriminate between squares and X’s: two birds were trained to report X stimuli as targets and to ignore square stimuli as distracters, while one bird was trained to report square stimuli as targets and to ignore X stimuli as distracters. When the target and distracter were in the same left or right hemifield, the birds were generally less accurate in choosing the target as opposed to when the target and distracter were in opposite hemifields. There was a large difference in the performance between different elevations. Data varied depending on visual field quadrant and the behavior of the bird itself. These data can provide implications about attention to stimuli, potentially leading to neural recordings that show the importance of the midbrain selection network for comparing the relative strengths of competing stimuli and selecting the one with the greatest importance for attention. An understanding of mechanisms underlying the brain’s ability to attend is needed to develop targeted therapies for psychiatric disorders, including ADHD, schizophrenia, bipolar disorder, and autism, for which there are no known cures.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
